<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tobacco use disorder, severe, on varenicline (F17.290)</content>. Down by about a third in two weeks on varenicline, before setting a quit date, which is the approach the ATS guideline supports. The nausea is mild and should ease if he takes it after food. [S]<list><item>Continue varenicline 1 mg twice daily after food.</item><item>Keep cutting down; choose a quit date at the next visit.</item><item>Enrolled with the quitline during the visit by the clinic&apos;s tobacco treatment nurse.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Maxillectomy defect with a worn obturator (Z90.09)</content>. Dr. Vasquez-Lindqvist found a worn, porous obturator that no longer seals at the back and a pressure ulcer at the front. She relined it at the chair today and relieved the flange; a new definitive obturator is planned. The ulcer looks traumatic and should heal within two weeks once the pressure is relieved. [S]<list><item>Ulcer rechecked in two weeks at the prosthodontics clinic; head and neck surgeon and biopsy if it has not healed.</item><item>Impressions for the new obturator in about four weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic pain at the maxillectomy site (G89.29)</content>. Pain 6 of 10, mostly where the flange presses. [S]<list><item>Continue acetaminophen 1,000 mg three times daily.</item><item>Warm saline rinses after meals.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Tobacco use (cigars a day), readiness and quit date</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About fourteen cigars a day, from twenty; quit date not yet chosen</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Varenicline adherence and side effects (nausea, sleep, dreams, mood)</td><td>Every visit; at least 12 weeks, extended to 24 weeks or more</td><td>Completed today</td><td>1 mg twice daily; mild nausea before breakfast, vivid dreams; no mood change; advised to take after food</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Behavioral support (quitline and clinic sessions)</td><td>Weekly calls around the quit date, then every two to four weeks</td><td>Deferred</td><td>He had not called the quitline in the two weeks since intake. No harm so far: he has cut down by a third. Corrective action: the tobacco treatment nurse enrolled him directly during the visit, and the quitline will call him this week</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Kidney function for varenicline dosing</td><td>Baseline; again if illness or new medicines</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Creatinine 0.96 mg/dL, eGFR 83 at intake; no dose change needed</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Head and neck surveillance examination (mouth, defect and neck)</td><td>Yearly by his head and neck surgeon more than five years after treatment; mouth checked at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Traumatic ulcer at the defect margin; no mass, induration or white or red patch; neck clear. Yearly surgeon&apos;s examination due in about two months</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Head and neck imaging</td><td>Only for symptoms or signs more than five years after treatment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Seven years after treatment with no symptoms of recurrence</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Obturator fit, seal and hygiene</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Worn, porous obturator with a poor posterior seal; relined and adjusted today by Dr. Vasquez-Lindqvist; new definitive obturator planned</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Speech and swallowing (hypernasality, nasal leakage)</td><td>Every visit; speech-language therapy as needed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hypernasal speech and nasal leakage before the reline; better with the relined obturator; speech-language therapy planned</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Dental care (high-fluoride toothpaste, cleaning every three months)</td><td>Daily toothpaste; cleaning every three months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>High-fluoride toothpaste advised by Dr. Vasquez-Lindqvist</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Osteoradionecrosis, trismus and neck fibrosis</td><td>Only after radiation to the head and neck</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No radiation in his treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Thyroid function</td><td>Only after radiation to the neck</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No neck radiation</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and nutrition</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>58.2 kg; soft high-protein diet and supplements twice daily</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain score and function</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6 of 10 before the reline</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acetaminophen daily dose (no more than 3,000 mg)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>3,000 mg a day; no other acetaminophen products</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Opioids</td><td>Avoid long-term opioids</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not needed; pain is mechanical and improving with prosthetic care</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Maxillofacial prosthodontics consultation</td><td>At a worn or poorly sealing obturator</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Vasquez-Lindqvist today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stop smoking cigars completely, at a pace he chooses, using varenicline and nicotine replacement. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>An obturator that seals and does not rub, so that speech is clear and liquids do not leak into his nose. In progress; target 2026-08-24 [S]</item><item>Pain at 3 of 10 or less without opioids. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>Regain weight to at least 60 kg with a normal albumin. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Initiating Pharmacologic Treatment in Tobacco-Dependent Adults. An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2020); Tobacco Smoking Cessation in Adults, Including Pregnant Persons: Interventions (2021); NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Head and Neck Cancers, Version 2.2026 (follow-up and survivorship) (2026); American Cancer Society Head and Neck Cancer Survivorship Care Guideline (2016); CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain — United States, 2022 (2022)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Quitline call this week. Six-week review in four weeks. Prosthodontic impressions for the new obturator in about four weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-13: six-week review: smoking down by two thirds, setting a quit date, pain and weight. [S]</paragraph></text>

